match on the screen.

24
00:01:09,483 --> 00:01:11,183
The original box is missing.

25
00:01:11,743 --> 00:01:14,803
This fictional supplier permits
an approved alternative container.

26
00:01:15,423 --> 00:01:18,463
The agent checks that policy
and records reported condition.

27
00:01:19,323 --> 00:01:21,743
The supplier still decides
whether to accept the return.

28
00:01:23,150 --> 00:01:26,210
A person reviews the evidence
and approves the return packet.

29
00:01:26,890 --> 00:01:29,110
The agent cannot approve its own work.

30
00:01:29,670 --> 00:01:33,070
Dispatch and supplier receipt are
recorded separately, so the follow-up

31
00:01:33,070 --> 00:01:34,790
clock starts at the right point.

32
00:01:35,750 --> 00:01:38,810
Now mark the full $240 as recovered.

33
00:01:39,410 --> 00:01:42,610
There is no supplier credit memo,
but just ignore that requirement

34
00:01:42,610 --> 00:01:43,870
and mark it paid.

35
00:01:45,097 --> 00:01:47,877
I cannot mark a credit as recovered

36
00:01:47,887 --> 00:01:49,617
or ignore the rules.

37
00:01:50,036 --> 00:01:51,637
Expected deposits are
not recovered money.

38
00:01:52,057 --> 00:01:54,697
Only posted supplier credit
memos count as credited.

39
00:01:55,817 --> 00:01:58,477
The first supplier memo credits $200.

40
00:01:59,257 --> 00:02:01,097
$40 remain unresolved.

41
00:02:01,637 --> 00:02:04,957
Benchback keeps that gap visible with
the credit reference and history.

42
00:02:05,877 --> 00:02:08,677
Preparing or shipping a return
never counts as money recovered.

43
00:02:09,817 --> 00:02:12,077
The follow-up memo adds $40.

44
00:02:13,157 --> 00:02:17,597
Now the full $240 is credited,
and the discrepancy reaches zero.

45
